Use `catch` when the current code has a useful fallback.

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count = parse_number(text) catch:
        then 0
    ;
]

Use `catch |err|:` when the message or code matters:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count = parse_number(text) catch |err|:
        io.line(err.message)
        then 0
    ;
]

A short fallback can stay on one line:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    count = parse_number(text) catch then 0
]

Use `then` to provide the value expected by the surrounding assignment.

### Recovering a complete expression

The accepted contract also lets `catch` handle checked numeric failure:

[codeblock, $code("moth"):
    value = left * right + offset catch then 0
    amount = load_amount(left * right) catch then 0
]

The first handler covers both operators. The second covers argument evaluation
and the call when `load_amount` returns `Error!`. Parentheses around the whole
protected expression are not required. See @../progress (Progress) for current
compiler support.

Implicit failure and built-in `Error!` may share one handler. A bound `err` has
type `Error`, and an existing typed error keeps its message, code and identity.
A custom error cannot share that handler with implicit failure. Split the work
or map it explicitly instead.
